aliasing a mutated dict is out of scope: `other = generics` binds a SECOND NAME TO THE SAME dict in Python, and a later store through one name is visible through the other. PyCSL models a dict as a pure map in a `ref`, so this lowers to `let other = ref !generics` — a COPY — and the write would be invisible (route #59). Refusing instead of answering incorrectly. A read-only rebind is fine; restructure so only one name mutates the dict.
